1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,840
We are discussing the system requirements for standard deployments of Burp Suite Enterprise

2
00:00:07,840 --> 00:00:09,260
Edition.

3
00:00:09,260 --> 00:00:12,900
Please note you should refer to the full documentation when designing your installation.

4
00:00:12,900 --> 00:00:16,920
In this video we will just cover general advice.

5
00:00:16,920 --> 00:00:21,120
To begin you can perform a standard deployment of Burp Suite Enterprise Edition on premise

6
00:00:21,120 --> 00:00:24,280
or on cloud hosted virtual machines.

7
00:00:24,280 --> 00:00:28,080
For general requirements please ensure that you meet the following conditions, that the

8
00:00:28,080 --> 00:00:33,080
application is self-hosted in your own environment, all the components are installed on machines

9
00:00:33,080 --> 00:00:39,120
that have a 64-bit architecture, the disk location must be locally attached storage rather than

10
00:00:39,120 --> 00:00:44,360
a network file system, and we do recommend that you use dedicated server class machines

11
00:00:44,360 --> 00:00:48,640
when deploying Burp Suite Enterprise.

12
00:00:48,640 --> 00:00:53,180
In terms of the operating systems available we do support Windows, Linux and Mac OS.

13
00:00:53,180 --> 00:00:57,160
You can refer to the full list of versions in the documentation here.

14
00:00:57,160 --> 00:01:02,720
But do note that we don't recommend CentOS or Red Hat Linux version 7, and typically we

15
00:01:02,720 --> 00:01:06,160
recommend the latest Ubuntu long-term support release.

16
00:01:06,160 --> 00:01:10,940
Additionally, if you do choose to deploy to Linux, depending on the OS that you use you

17
00:01:10,940 --> 00:01:15,220
may need to install packages to allow BIP's Chromium browser to run.

18
00:01:15,220 --> 00:01:19,560
These again depend on the Linux distribution that you've chosen, but we do have documentation

19
00:01:19,560 --> 00:01:24,160
which can guide you through this process under browser-powered scanning.

20
00:01:24,160 --> 00:01:29,160
Next you must decide if you want to deploy to a single machine deployment or multi-machine.

21
00:01:29,160 --> 00:01:33,160
Single machine is deploying all the components to, as the name describes, a single machine.

22
00:01:33,160 --> 00:01:39,160
Everything is contained as one, and this architecture allows you to run up to five concurrent scans.

23
00:01:39,160 --> 00:01:44,720
We typically only recommend this deployment for POC or trial purposes.

24
00:01:44,720 --> 00:01:51,720
You can see when referencing our documentation that we have a minimum specification relative to the number of scans that you want to run,

25
00:01:51,720 --> 00:01:55,720
and a recommended specification again relative to the number of scans that you want to run.

26
00:01:55,720 --> 00:02:03,720
If your applications, in terms of your target scans, are fairly small and static, you're fine to go with the minimum recommendations.

27
00:02:03,720 --> 00:02:11,720
But for anything that is larger, dynamic, JavaScript heavy, we would advise that you go for the recommended specifications.

28
00:02:11,720 --> 00:02:17,720
We also advise on AWS EC2 instances, again based on those number of concurrent scans.

29
00:02:18,720 --> 00:02:21,720
For production deployments, we do recommend multi-machine deployments.

30
00:02:21,720 --> 00:02:26,720
This is where we separate out the enterprise server machine and the scanning machines to their own servers.

31
00:02:26,720 --> 00:02:29,720
This is great for scale and performance.

32
00:02:29,720 --> 00:02:35,720
So you'll see a reference to the enterprise server itself, and again in terms of a minimum or recommended specification level.

33
00:02:35,720 --> 00:02:41,720
And then the same goes for your scanning machines, again relative to how many scans you would like to run.

34
00:02:41,720 --> 00:02:52,720
So for example, I could deploy a scanning machine configured to run five concurrent scans, and deploy another scanning machine configured to run five concurrent scans,

35
00:02:52,720 --> 00:02:54,720
and the specification would match here.

36
00:02:58,720 --> 00:03:02,720
Please note that if you do need any help with the system requirements or designing your deployment,

37
00:03:02,720 --> 00:03:07,720
you can contact our support team at support at portswigger.net, and we will endeavor to assist you.

38
00:03:07,720 --> 00:03:08,720
Thank you.

39
00:03:11,720 --> 00:03:12,720
Thank you.

